### What is the subarrays with product less than target problem?

Given an array of positive numbers and a target, count the number of contiguous subarrays whose product is strictly less than the target. This is a sliding-window counting problem that works because all numbers are positive, which guarantees the product changes monotonically as the window grows and shrinks.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SubarrayProductLessThanK {

    public static int countSubarrays(int[] arr, int target) {
        if (target <= 1) return 0;

        int count = 0;
        int product = 1;
        int left = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < arr.length; right++) {
            product *= arr[right];

            while (product >= target && left <= right) {
                product /= arr[left];
                left++;
            }

            count += right - left + 1;
        }
        return count;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] multipliers = {10, 5, 2, 6};
        int target = 100;
        System.out.println("Count: " + countSubarrays(multipliers, target));
    }
}
```

```text
Count: 8
```

### Why does the window length give the count of valid subarrays?

When the window is `[left, right]` and the product is below the target, every subarray that ends at `right` and starts anywhere from `left` to `right` is also valid. That is because removing elements from the left can only decrease the product (since all elements are positive), so shorter subarrays within the window also have products below the target. The count of such subarrays is `right - left + 1`.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class WindowLengthCount {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {10, 5, 2, 6};
        int target = 100;
        int product = 1, left = 0, count = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < arr.length; right++) {
            product *= arr[right];
            while (product >= target && left <= right) {
                product /= arr[left];
                left++;
            }
            int windowLen = right - left + 1;
            System.out.println("right=" + right + " window=[" + left + "," + right
                    + "] product=" + product + " new subarrays=" + windowLen);
            count += windowLen;
        }
        System.out.println("Total: " + count);
    }
}
```

```text
right=0 window=[0,0] product=10 new subarrays=1
right=1 window=[0,1] product=50 new subarrays=2
right=2 window=[0,2] product=100 new subarrays=2
right=3 window=[1,3] product=60 new subarrays=3
Total: 8
```

### Why does the shrink loop divide out from the left?

Multiplying in a new right element can push the product above the target. The only way to bring it back down is to remove elements from the left by dividing them out. Because all elements are positive, each division strictly reduces the product. We keep dividing until the product drops back below the target or the window becomes empty.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class ShrinkDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {10, 5, 2, 6};
        int target = 100;
        int product = 1, left = 0;

        // Simulate reaching right=2: product = 10*5*2 = 100
        for (int i = 0; i <= 2; i++) product *= arr[i];
        System.out.println("Before shrink: product=" + product + " left=" + left);

        while (product >= target) {
            System.out.println("  Dividing out arr[" + left + "]=" + arr[left]);
            product /= arr[left];
            left++;
        }
        System.out.println("After shrink: product=" + product + " left=" + left);
    }
}
```

```text
Before shrink: product=100 left=0
  Dividing out arr[0]=10
After shrink: product=10 left=1
```

### Why do all elements need to be positive?

The sliding window works because multiplying a positive number always increases the product and dividing always decreases it. If a zero appeared, the product would drop to zero and never recover through division. If a negative appeared, multiplying could flip the sign, breaking the monotonic property. In those cases, a different approach such as prefix products in log space would be needed.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class PositivityRequirement {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // With all positives: monotonic product
        int product = 1;
        int[] positive = {2, 3, 4};
        for (int val : positive) {
            product *= val;
            System.out.println("After multiplying " + val + ": product=" + product);
        }

        // With a zero: product collapses
        product = 1;
        int[] withZero = {2, 0, 4};
        for (int val : withZero) {
            product *= val;
            System.out.println("After multiplying " + val + ": product=" + product);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
After multiplying 2: product=2
After multiplying 3: product=6
After multiplying 4: product=24
After multiplying 2: product=2
After multiplying 0: product=0
After multiplying 4: product=0
```

### What happens when the target is 1 or less?

If the target is 1 or less, no subarray of positive integers (each at least 1) can have a product below it. We return zero immediately. This edge case guard also prevents the shrink loop from producing incorrect counts.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class TargetEdgeCase {

    public static int countSubarrays(int[] arr, int target) {
        if (target <= 1) return 0;  // Guard: no positive product < 1
        int count = 0, product = 1, left = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < arr.length; right++) {
            product *= arr[right];
            while (product >= target && left <= right) {
                product /= arr[left]; left++;
            }
            count += right - left + 1;
        }
        return count;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("target=0: " + countSubarrays(new int[]{1, 2, 3}, 0));
        System.out.println("target=1: " + countSubarrays(new int[]{1, 2, 3}, 1));
    }
}
```

```text
target=0: 0
target=1: 0
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include a single-element array, all elements equal to 1, and a target larger than the product of the entire array.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class ProductEdgeCases {

    public static int countSubarrays(int[] arr, int target) {
        if (target <= 1) return 0;
        int count = 0, product = 1, left = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < arr.length; right++) {
            product *= arr[right];
            while (product >= target && left <= right) { product /= arr[left]; left++; }
            count += right - left + 1;
        }
        return count;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Single below: " + countSubarrays(new int[]{5}, 10));
        System.out.println("Single at: " + countSubarrays(new int[]{10}, 10));
        System.out.println("All ones: " + countSubarrays(new int[]{1, 1, 1}, 2));
        System.out.println("Huge target: " + countSubarrays(new int[]{2, 3, 4}, 1000));
    }
}
```

```text
Single below: 1
Single at: 0
All ones: 6
Huge target: 6
```

### How is this different from subarray sum problems?

Subarray sum problems use a similar sliding-window approach but add and subtract instead of multiply and divide. The key difference is that product-based windows require strictly positive elements for monotonicity, while sum-based windows require non-negative elements.

| Aspect | Product window | Sum window |
|---|---|---|
| Grow operation | Multiply | Add |
| Shrink operation | Divide | Subtract |
| Monotonic when | All elements positive | All elements non-negative |
| Breaks when | Zeros or negatives present | Negatives present |

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know that the linear-time sliding window depends entirely on the positivity of the elements. They should mention the edge case guard for target <= 1, the left <= right guard in the shrink loop, and the fact that integer overflow is possible if the product of the window exceeds `Integer.MAX_VALUE` before the shrink loop fires. Using `long` for the product is a safe practice.
